"Positive matching decompositions of the cartesian product of graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02826)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Title: Positive matching decompositions of the cartesian product of graphs ... A positive matching decomposition (pmd) of $\\Gamma$ with $p$ parts is an ordered partition $E_1,\\ldots,E_p$ of $E$ such that $E_i$ is a positive matching of $(V, E \\setminus \\bigcup_{j=1}^{i-1} E_j)$, for $i = 1, \\ldots, p$.\nTitle: Positive matching decompositions of the cartesian product of graphs\nAuthors: Mohammad Farrokhi Derakhshandeh Ghouchan, Ali Akbar Yazdan Pour\nDate: Wed Feb  5 02:07:41 2025\n\nLet $\\Gamma=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. A matching $M \\subseteq E$ is positive if there exists a weight function on $V$ such that the matching $M$ is characterized by those edges with positive weights. A positive matching decomposition (pmd) of $\\Gamma$ with $p$ parts is an ordered partition $E_1,\\ldots,E_p$ of $E$ such that $E_i$ is a positive matching of $(V, E \\setminus \\bigcup_{j=1}^{i-1} E_j)$, for $i = 1, \\ldots, p$. The smallest $p$ for which $\\Gamma$ admits a pmd with $p$ parts is denoted by $\\mathrm{pmd}(\\Gamma)$. We study the pmd of the Cartesian product of graphs and give sharp upper bounds for them in terms of the pmds and chromatic numbers of their components. In special cases, we compute the pmd of grid graphs that is the Cartesian product of paths and cycles.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPositive matching decompositions of graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X22001755)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In what follows, “the pmd” of a graph $\\Gamma $ denotes the minimum size of all positive matching decompositions of $\\Gamma $, namely $\\mathrm{pmd}\\left(\\Gamma \\right)$, while “a pmd” of $\\Gamma $ refers to any positive matching decomposition of $\\Gamma $.\n\n## Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 320, 30 October 2022, Pages 311-323\n\n# Positive matching decompositions of graphs\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2022.05.012Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nA matching $M$ in a graph $\\Gamma $ is positive if $\\Gamma $ has a vertex-labeling such that $M$ coincides with the set of edges with positive weights. A positive matching decomposition (pmd) of $\\Gamma $ is an edge-partition ${M}_{1},\\dots ,{M}_{p}$ of $\\Gamma $ such that ${M}_{i}$ is a positive matching in $\\Gamma -({M}_{1}\\cup \\cdots \\cup {M}_{i-1})$, for $i=1,\\dots ,p$. The pmds of graphs are used to study algebraic properties of the Lovász–Saks–Schrijver ideals arising from orthogonal representations of graphs. We give a characterization of pmds of graphs in terms of alternating closed walks and apply it to study pmds of various classes of graphs including complete multipartite graphs, (regular) bipartite graphs, cacti, generalized Petersen graphs, etc. We further show that computation of pmds of a graph can be reduced to that of its maximum pendant-free subgraph.\n\n## Introduction\n\nLet $\\left[n\\right]=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ and $\\Gamma =(\\left[n\\right],E)$ be a simple graph. We denote by $\\stackrel{̄}{\\Gamma }$ the complementary graph of $\\Gamma $ with the vertex set $\\left[n\\right]$ and edge set $\\stackrel{̄}{E}=\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{\\left[n\\right]}{2}\\right)\\setminus E$. An orthogonal representation of $\\Gamma $ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{d}$ assigns to each $i\\in \\left[n\\right]$ a vector ${u}_{i}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{d}$ such that ${u}_{i}^{T}{u}_{j}=0$, whenever $\\{i,j\\}\\in \\stackrel{̄}{E}$. Clearly, every graph $\\Gamma =(\\left[n\\right],E)$ has a trivial orthogonal representation in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{n}$, in which the vertex $i$ is represented by the standard basis vector ${e}_{i}$. More generally, if $\\{{X}_{1},\\dots ,{X}_{\\chi }\\}$ is the coloring classes of $\\stackrel{̄}{\\Gamma }$, then $\\Gamma $ has an orthogonal representation in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{\\chi }$, by assigning $x\\in {X}_{i}$ to ${e}_{i}$ [8, Example 10.5].\n\nDefinition [2, Definition 5.1]\n\nGiven a graph $\\Gamma =(V,E)$, a positive matching of $\\Gamma $ is a matching $M$ of $\\Gamma $ such that there exists a weight function $w:V\\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfying: $\\sum _{v\\in e}w\\left(v\\right)>0,\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\text{ if }e\\in M,\\sum _{v\\in e}w\\left(v\\right)<0,\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\text{ if }e\\notin M.$\n\nRemark\n\nNotice that in the definition of positive matchings we may replace real labels with integers by multiplying all labels by a large enough positive integer and replacing new labels with their integer parts.\n\nDefinition [2, Definition 5.3]\n\nLet $\\Gamma =(V,E)$ be a graph. A positive matching decomposition (or pmd) of $\\Gamma $ is a partition $E={\\bigcup }_{i=1}^{p}{E}_{i}$ of $E$ into pairwise disjoint subsets such that ${E}_{i}$ is a positive matching of $(V,E\\setminus {\\bigcup }_{j=1}^{i-1}{E}_{j})$, for $i=1,\\dots ,p$. The ${E}_{i}$ are called the parts of the pmd. The smallest $p$ for which $\\Gamma $ admits a pmd with $p$ parts will be denoted by $\\mathrm{pmd}\\left(\\Gamma \\right)$.\n\nIn what follows, “the pmd” of a graph $\\Gamma $ denotes the minimum size of all positive matching decompositions of $\\Gamma $, namely $\\mathrm{pmd}\\left(\\Gamma \\right)$, while “a pmd” of $\\Gamma $ refers to any positive matching decomposition of $\\Gamma $. Also, it is evident from the definition that a matching $M$ of a graph $\\Gamma $ is positive in $\\Gamma $ if and only if it is positive in $\\Gamma \\left[M\\right]$, the subgraph of $\\Gamma $ induced by vertices of $M$.\n\nThe following theorem establishes a nice connection between the pmd of a graph with algebraic properties of the corresponding LSS-ideal.\n\nTheorem 1.2 [2, Theorem 1.3]\n\nLet $\\Gamma $ be a graph. Then for $d\\ge \\mathrm{pmd}\\left(\\Gamma \\right)$ the ideal ${L}_{\\Gamma }^{\\mathbb{K}}\\left(d\\right)$ is a radical complete intersection ideal. In particular, ${L}_{\\Gamma }^{\\mathbb{K}}\\left(d\\right)$ is prime if $d\\ge \\mathrm{pmd}\\left(\\Gamma \\right)+1$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Literature Review] Positive matching decompositions of the cartesian product of graphs (https://www.themoonlight.io/en/review/positive-matching-decompositions-of-the-cartesian-product-of-graphs)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Proposition 4.1 shows $4 \\le \\mathrm{pmd}(P_m \\times C_n) \\le 5$4≤pmd(P m​×C n​)≤5 for $m, n \\ge 3$m,n≥3, providing conditions for when it equals 4 ($n$n even and $n \\ge 4(m-1)$n≥4(m−1), or $n$n odd and $n > 2m$n>2 m). Lower bounds are discussed using Lemma 4.3: for an $r$r-regular graph ($r \\ge 2$r≥2), $\\mathrm{pmd}(\\Gamma) \\ge r+1$pmd(Γ)≥r+1.\nSection 4 computes the pmd for grid graphs. Known results include $\\mathrm{pmd}(P_2 \\times P_n) = 3$pmd(P 2​×P n​)=3, $\\mathrm{pmd}(P_2 \\times C_n) = 4$pmd(P 2​×C n​)=4 ($n \\ne 4$n=4), and $\\mathrm{pmd}(P_2 \\times C_4) = 5$pmd(P 2​×C 4​)=5. For $m, n \\ge 3$m,n≥3, Corollary 2.4 gives $\\mathrm{pmd}(P_m \\times P_n) = 4$pmd(P m​×P n​)=4. Proposition 4.1 shows $4 \\le \\mathrm{pmd}(P_m \\times C_n) \\le 5$4≤pmd(P m​×C n​)≤5 for $m, n \\ge 3$m,n≥3, providing conditions for when it equals 4 ($n$n even and $n \\ge 4(m-1)$n≥4(m−1), or $n$n odd and $n > 2m$n>2 m). Lower bounds are discussed using Lemma 4.3: for an $r$r-regular graph ($r \\ge 2$r≥2), $\\mathrm{pmd}(\\Gamma) \\ge r+1$pmd(Γ)≥r+1. The circular wall graph $CW(m, n)$C W(m,n) is introduced as a subgraph of $P_m \\times C_n$P m​×C n​. Proposition 4.4 computes its pmd for $m \\ge 2$m≥2 and even $n \\ge 4$n≥4: $\\mathrm{pmd}(CW(m, n)) = 3$pmd(C W(m,n))=3 if $n > 2m$n>2 m and 4 if $n \\le 2m$n≤2 m. Finally, Theorem 4.5 analyzes $\\mathrm{pmd}(C_m \\times C_n)$pmd(C m​×C n​) for $m, n \\ge 3$m,n≥3: $5 \\le \\mathrm{pmd}